<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Alsace</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Alsace is a historic region of north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vosges">Vosges</a> mountains and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>, with Strasbourg as its main city. Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a> for centuries, it passed largely to France between 1648 and 1681, was annexed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> in 1871 as part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alsace-lorraine">Alsace-Lorraine</a> and returned to France in 1918, apart from a further German annexation from 1940 to 1944. Its Alemannic dialect, wines and half-timbered towns reflect this mixed heritage.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alsace.jpg" alt="Alsace" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jorge Franganillo from Barcelona, Spain · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Colmar_(32350846618).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Alsace is a plain on the west bank of the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>, between the river and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vosges">Vosges</a> mountains, almost four times longer than it is wide. It covers 8,283 square kilometres and includes the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">departments</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haut-rhin">Haut-Rhin</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bas-rhin">Bas-Rhin</a>, formerly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sundgau">Sundgau</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nordgau">Nordgau</a>. Until 1871 it also included the area now called the Territoire de Belfort. The plain is a rift valley dating from the Oligocene, and the Vosges shelter it from rain; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colmar">Colmar</a> is the second driest city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, a condition suited to the Alsatian wine. The name comes from Old High German Ali-saz, meaning foreign domain, or perhaps from Ell-sass, seated on the Ill.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Hominids lived in Alsace 600,000 years ago, and farming arrived with the Linear Pottery culture by 4000 BCE. In 58 BCE Julius Caesar routed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suebi">Suebi</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ariovistus">Ariovistus</a> at the foot of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vosges">Vosges</a>, near what became Cernay. The region lay in the Roman province of Germania Superior. As Roman power declined it passed to the Alemanni, whose language forms the basis of the dialects of the Upper Rhine, and in the 5th century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clovis-i">Clovis</a> defeated them. Alsace was then part of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austrasia">Austrasia</a>. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-verdun">Treaty of Verdun</a> of 843 it fell to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-francia">Middle Francia</a>, ruled by Lothar I, and later it prospered under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hohenstaufen">Hohenstaufen</a> emperors in the 12th and 13th centuries.</p><p>Within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> took most of Alsace in the 17th century. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> sold their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sundgau">Sundgau</a> territory to France in 1646 for 1.2 million thalers, and the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westphalia">Westphalia</a> of 1648 recognised most of Alsace as French, though some towns stayed independent. The inhabitants kept their existing rights and customs, German remained the language of administration, schools and the Lutheran University of Strasbourg, and the Edict of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fontainebleau">Fontainebleau</a> of 1685 was not applied there. Strasbourg Cathedral, Lutheran from 1524 to 1681, returned to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-church">Catholic Church</a>.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a> divided Alsace into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">departments</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haut-rhin">Haut-Rhin</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bas-rhin">Bas-Rhin</a>. In 1792 Rouget de Lisle composed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-marseillaise">La Marseillaise</a> in Strasbourg for the Army of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>. In 1871 Alsace became part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a> as part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alsace-lorraine">Alsace-Lorraine</a>. It was returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, reincorporated into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> in 1940, and became French again after 1944.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Alsace has a population of 1,919,745 in 2021, and its capital and largest city is Strasbourg. The Alsatian language is an Alemannic dialect, though most people mainly speak French. Under the local law of Alsace-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moselle">Moselle</a>, the Napoleonic Concordat of 1801 still provides public support for the Catholic, Lutheran, Calvinist and Jewish faiths. The region is known for vineyards along the Route des Vins d'Alsace, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a>-framed houses and historic towns such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colmar">Colmar</a> and Sélestat.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Alsace” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Valentinian I fought them again after the king Rando sacked Mainz in 368, winning a hard battle at Solicinium, and in 378 Gratian defeated the Lentienses near Argentaria in <b>Alsace</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/alemanni">Alemanni · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1525 Duke Antoine destroyed a peasant army of the German Peasants’ War near Saverne in <b>Alsace</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-lorraine">Duchy of Lorraine · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>After the surrender of January 1871, a conservative National Assembly made Adolphe Thiers head of the executive and signed the Treaty of Frankfurt on 10 May 1871, ceding <b>Alsace</b> and most of Lorraine to the new German Empire.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-third-republic">French Third Republic · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Germany occupied northern France and the whole Atlantic coast and in effect annexed <b>Alsace</b> and Lorraine, leaving about two-fifths of metropolitan France and the empire to the French government.</blockquote><a href="/empire/vichy-france">Vichy France · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The republic's foreign policy was shaped by the Treaty of Versailles, under which Germany lost about 13% of its territory and 7 million people, its colonies and <b>Alsace</b>–Lorraine, and accepted the War Guilt Clause.</blockquote><a href="/empire/weimar-republic">Weimar Republic · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Franco-Prussian War, in which Bartholdi served as a major of militia and his home province of <b>Alsace</b> was lost, he crossed the Atlantic in June 1871 with letters of introduction from Laboulaye.</blockquote><a href="/work/statue-of-liberty">Statue of Liberty · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
